  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Friday)\nIssued at 1215 PM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nAn overall \"cooler\" day is in play across the Mid-South with\ncurrent temperatures largely in the 80s as a decaying MCV rotates\njust west of the Memphis metropolitan area. The latest 500mb\nanalysis indicates a shortwave trough collocated with the\naforementioned MCV. Taking a look at KNQA radar, it is currently\ndepicting a few pop-up thunderstorms across many areas over the\nMid-South with lift from the aforementioned shortwave. As the\nshortwave transverses our area and we move into the afternoon\nhours, when peak heating occurs, a slight uptick in shower and\nweak thunderstorm coverage is anticipated with our typical summer\nday. Overall coverage is expected to begin to slowly taper off\nafter sunset as the mid-level feature begins to push further east\nand over into Nashville's area.  As far as temperatures go,\nincreased cloud cover and shower and thunderstorm coverage has\nresulted in us falling just shy of Heat Advisory criteria (105+F\nheat indices) today as highs top out in the upper 80s to lower\n90s.\n\nHeading into the overnight hours, there is a decent signal for\nsome patchy fog, especially in areas that see precipitation today\nand low-lying areas, as winds light and calm along a weak low-\nlevel inversion. Any fog that does form should burn off shortly\nafter sunrise as winds begin to increase. Residual moisture and a\nfew embedded shortwave perturbations will allow for very isolated\nshower coverage tomorrow morning, increasing in the afternoon\nhours as they become largely diurnally driven. Tomorrow also\nlooks to be another cooler one as lingering cloud cover and the\noverall upper-level pattern remains much the same as today with\nhighs in the upper 80s to lower 90s. Though some areas in the\nDelta Region may approach heat indices around 105F, confidence in\nwidespread impacts are not anticipated, not warranting the\nissuance of a Heat Advisory tomorrow. Monday on the other hand,\nwill be a different story as the pattern aloft turns quasi-zonal\nas ridging begins to broaden over the southeastern CONUS.\nTemperatures will be increased with highs in the low 90s with\nincreased dewpoints in the mid to upper 70s thanks to increased\nmoisture and PWATs lingering around 1.8\". NBM dewpoints may be\nslightly overdone, however, heat indices 105+F are anticipated\nacross at least some locations here in the Mid-South, warranting\na Heat Advisory.\n\nThe pattern won't change very much moving through midweek as the\naforementioned ridge continues to broaden slightly over the\nsoutheastern CONUS and we remain in largely zonal flow aloft.\nTuesday and Wednesday will be a near rinse and repeat of Monday,\njust a little warmer both days, potentially reaching the mid 90s\nby Wednesday. By the latter half of the week, ensembles begin to\nplace us into more of a northwest flow regime aloft as dual upper-\nlevel troughs begin to dig, one over the Pacific Northwest and\nthe other over the northern Great Lakes Region. As this\ntransition occurs aloft, a slight uptick in shower and\nthunderstorm coverage is expected, however, how widespread\ncoverage will be remains unresolved as this will greatly depend\non where exactly the aforementioned upper-lows set up. One thing\nto note: how the latter half of next week plays out will largely\ninfluence temperatures as there is currently about a 5 degree\nspread. For now, we are heading into mid August so be prepared\nfor warm and humid weather to linger.\n\nAEH",
